---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import RateCalculator from '../../islands/RateCalculator';

const faq: [string, string][] = [
  ['How do I calculate my freelance hourly rate?', 'Add the take-home pay you want to your yearly business costs and the income tax you will owe, add a safety margin for quiet months, then divide by the hours you can actually bill in a year. Billable hours are far fewer than the hours you work, because sales, admin and holidays are unpaid.'],
  ['How many billable hours a week is realistic?', 'Most full-time freelancers bill 20 to 30 hours a week. The rest goes on finding clients, writing proposals, invoicing, learning and admin. Planning on 40 billable hours makes your rate come out too low.'],
  ['Should I charge by the hour, by the day or per project?', 'Hourly suits work with an unclear scope. Day rates suit consulting and contract work. Per-project pricing usually pays best once you know how long the work takes, because you keep the gain when you get faster. Whichever you use, start from your minimum hourly rate so you know which deals are worth taking.'],
  ['What is a safety margin?', 'An extra percentage on top of what you need, to cover months with less work, clients who pay late, and unexpected costs. 10 to 20 percent is common.'],
  ['Does this calculator store my numbers?', 'Only in your own browser, so they are there next time. Nothing is sent to a server.'],
];
---
<ToolLayout
  slug="freelance-hourly-rate-calculator"
  title="Freelance Rate Calculator: What Should I Charge per Hour? | SoloKit"
  description="Free freelance hourly rate calculator. Enter your income goal, costs, tax and billable hours to get your minimum hourly and day rate, and check your current rate."
  h1="Freelance rate calculator"
  lede="Find the minimum hourly and day rate that pays for tax, business costs and time off. Then check whether your current rate gets you there."
  trust={['Free, no sign-up', 'Any currency', 'Saved only on your device']}
  faq={faq}
>
  <RateCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="article">
    <h2>How your rate is worked out</h2>
    <p>Most new freelancers take their old salary, divide by about 2,000 working hours and wonder why money is short by the spring. A freelance rate has to pay for things an employer used to cover: tax, equipment, software, holidays and all the hours you spend on work no one pays for.</p>
    <p class="formula">Hourly rate = (take-home ÷ (1 − tax rate) + business costs) × (1 + safety margin) ÷ (billable hours per week × weeks worked)</p>
    <p>With the starting numbers, a take-home goal of $60,000 at a 25% tax rate needs $80,000 of profit. Add $6,000 of costs and a 10% margin and you need to invoice $94,600 a year. Billing 25 hours a week for 46 weeks gives 1,150 hours, so the minimum is $83 an hour, or $664 a day.</p>

    <h2>Getting each number right</h2>
    <h3>Take-home pay</h3>
    <p>Use what you need to live on and save, after tax. If you're leaving a job, your old net salary is a fair start. Include retirement savings and health cover if your employer used to pay for them.</p>
    <h3>Business costs</h3>
    <p>Count everything it takes to run your business for a year: laptop and phone spread over their life, software, internet, a coworking desk, insurance, an accountant and payment fees. Use "Help me add these up" for a quick list.</p>
    <h3>Tax rate</h3>
    <p>Use your average (effective) rate: the total tax you pay divided by your profit. It's lower than your top tax band. If you're not sure, 20–30% is a common planning figure; an accountant in your country can give you a precise one.</p>
    <h3>Billable hours and weeks off</h3>
    <p>This is the number that moves the result most, so be honest. Count holidays, public holidays and a few sick days as weeks off, then count only the hours a client pays for.</p>

    <h2>Your rate is a floor, not a price</h2>
    <p>The result is the least you can charge and still meet your goal. Charge more for rush jobs, specialist skills, high-value outcomes or clients who need a lot of meetings. When you know your floor, it's easier to say no to work that won't pay your bills.</p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
